Summary of important user-visible changes for drafting 0.2.2:
-------------------------------------------------------------------

 ** New method `solid.Shape.thread (S, P, NAME, DEPTH)`: cuts an ISO metric
    thread, internal in a bore or external on a cylinder or tube.

 ** New method `solid.Shape.knurl (S, F, PITCH)`: knurls a cylindrical face
    in every type of DIN 82, straight, single or crossed, with raised or
    indented points, and a fillet or chamfer at a free end.

 ** New functions `solid.threadspec (NAME)`, the dimensions of a metric
    thread, and `solid.holespec (NAME, ...)`, the hole drilled for a metric
    screw, tapped or clear.

 ** `solid.Shape.hole` takes any ISO 261 thread from M1 to M64, fine ones
    as `'M10x1.25'`, and any diameter and pitch with `'Custom', true`.  It
    drills clearance holes by ISO 273 with the standard counterbores and
    countersinks of IS 3406, places a hole at each point of `'At'`, and
    chooses the drill in the 6H band with `'Material'` and `'Engagement'`.

 ** New property `Name` of `solid.Shape`, a name for each solid, kept by
    transforms and booleans and written to STEP and 3MF.  New methods
    `solid.Shape.solids` and `model.Assembly.solids` list each solid's
    index, name and colour.

 ** `model.Viewer` gains `capture`, the picture it shows, and `opacity`,
    which hides solids or shows them through; `pick (V, 'solid')` picks
    whole solids, `view` takes a zoom and a target, and the mouse turns the
    view keeping z up, the T key any way.  The property `Shape` is renamed
    `Model` and takes a `model.Assembly`; `Shape` works with a warning
    until 0.3.0.

 ** `line`, `point`, `arc`, `circle`, `ellipse`, `centremark` and `text` of
    `draw.Drawing` append one entity per row of their points.

 ** Large drawings are much faster to plot, print, merge, hatch, and read
    and write as DXF, often ten times or more; `geom.largestrect` too.
    `solid.Shape.union` keeps a solid that meets no other as it is: seven
    knurled knobs side by side took 95 s and take 1 s.

 ** The package builds against Open CASCADE 8.0 as well as 7.8 and later,
    finds it and X11 where distributions and source builds install them,
    with new `OCCT_LIB` and `X11_LIB` to set them by hand, and runs against
    one installed outside the system's library path.

 ** Every true or false input must be logical, every input holding
    geometry floating point, and an empty value for a Name/Value option
    that would mean the option left out is an error.

 ** The guide gains tutorials on holes and threads (16), clearance holes
    (17), looking inside a model (18) and knurling (19), and a Standards
    section on threads, holes and knurls.

 ** Bug fixes:
    - `solid.Shape.subtract` and `intersect` returned the part uncut, or
      nothing, with a shape whose solids overlap, as an assembly's.
    - `solid.Shape.subtract`, and `pocket`, `hole` and `thread` with it,
      raise an error where Open CASCADE 7.8 drops a cut silently.
    - `solid.Shape.union` dropped an operand of several solids made by
      `solid.surface`.
    - `solid.Shape.hole` refuses a hole that misses the part.
    - A `solid.Shape` holds solids only: `solid.read` and `model.read` leave
      out surfaces, curves and points with a warning, on which booleans
      failed or were wrong.
    - `model.read` failed on STEP names that repeat or are missing and on a
      3MF placement after a scaled or mirrored one, copied a part placed
      with a six-digit rotation, and named the build `_2` after a part.
    - `model.Assembly.tessellate` dropped the vertex colours of meshes;
      `model.Assembly.add` refuses an empty sub-assembly.
    - `model.Viewer.Model` lost `Colour` and `Name`, and a pick on an
      assembly returned code that could not run.
    - Octave crashed on exit after meshing a solid where Octave builds
      packages with link-time optimisation, as Ubuntu's does, and crashed
      or hung on damaged DXF, PLY or 3MF input.
    - `polymesh.Mesh.volume` cancelled two shells turned opposite ways; it
      counts each shell, and one inside another as a cavity.
    - `polymesh.read` read a binary STL padded after its triangles as
      ASCII; it skips OBJ lines and points and PLY edges with a warning.
    - `draw.linetype` held inch values as millimetres, so every dash was
      25.4 times too short; `print` and `tikz` drew dashes and dimension
      text far too small at any scale but 1:1; a line of more than 1e5
      dashes stopped partway.
    - `draw.Drawing.transform` moved only the points of inserts, ellipses,
      arcs and dimensions, so inserts landed in the wrong place and a
      mirrored angle measured its explement; it refuses to mirror an
      insert.  Dimensions given no label kept the one measured when made.
    - `draw.Drawing.bbox` took an ellipse's centre, a bulge's chord and an
      insert's point.
    - Colours 9 to 255 were drawn mid grey, and `tikz` dropped the colour
      of text.
    - `plot` and `print` read braces and underscores in text as TeX and
      failed on a line weight of 0; `plot` failed under `hold` and takes a
      figure handle; `print` wrote a blank page with every layer hidden.
    - `hatch` took an unknown pattern and `angdim` arms on one ray.
    - `draw.read` placed blocks by the origin instead of their base point,
      one insert for a grid of rows and columns, and mirrored inserts
      wrongly, which it now skips with a warning; it read uncommon
      `$INSUNITS` as millimetres and failed on a byte-order mark or bytes
      after `EOF`.
    - DXF names outside ASCII came back garbled and are written as
      `\U+XXXX`; a line break in a name or text, or two blocks of one
      name, is an error on writing; dimension pictures clashed with a
      kept `*D` block.
    - `geom.signedarea` and `centroid` lost the area far from the origin,
      `intersectlines` crossed nearly parallel lines near 1e15,
      `hatchlines` made pieces of no length, `simplify` dropped a curve's
      turn back, `curveoffset` missed a negative offset folding, `offset`
      let the outline cross itself, and `selfintersects` reported a
      crossing at a repeated point.
    - A nearly flat arc made a region refuse its outline, and
      `geom.Polyline.fillet` and `chamfer` left a repeated vertex.
    - `polymesh.read` names a vertex that is not finite, and `geom.UCS`
      refuses extra arguments.


Backwards incompatible improvements:

 ** `geom.triangulate` is removed; `tessellate (solid.extrude (R, H))`
    triangulates a region exactly, holes included.

 ** `solid.Shape.hole` with a thread name drills the tapping drill of ISO
    2306, M8 at 6.8 instead of 6.75, countersinks a tapped hole at each
    open end, and ends a blind one in a 118 degree drill point;
    `'Countersink', false` and `'Tip', false` drill as before.

 ** The bytes a `solid.Shape` holds end with a check; damaged bytes, and
    bytes saved from 0.2.1, are refused.

 ** `draw.Drawing.plot` returns one handle per layer and appearance, plus
    one per text, instead of one per entity and per dash.
